    "perplexity": {
      "description": "The Truth is the first trio album from James Taylor, the British jazz organist and long-running Acid Jazz figure, and it was released on Acid Jazz as part of the label’s Jazz Sessions series in 2026. Framed as a celebration of Taylor’s 40th year as a solo artist, the record finds him in a leaner, more intimate setting than the James Taylor Quartet, with Theo Carasco on drums and Orlando Flemming on upright bass.\n\nMusically, the album draws on early-1970s soul-jazz and spiritual jazz, with a warm groove-led approach that recalls Les McCann, Hampton Hawes, and Lonnie Liston Smith. Its program includes reworkings such as “Linus and Lucy” and “Thus Sprach Zarathustra,” alongside original pieces like the title track and “Aref’s Space,” balancing familiar hooks with expansive, cosmic improvisation. The Truth was positioned as a significant return to Acid Jazz for Taylor, linking his roots in the label’s history with a fresh trio format that highlighted his improvisational voice.",
